1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,480
In the spring of 1943, a convoy crossing

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:05,700
the North Atlantic could sail beyond the

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:07,120
reach of its air cover.

4
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:11,040
The escort ships stayed. The aircraft had

5
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:11,920
to turn back.

6
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:16,120
For German U-boats, that gap offered room

7
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,040
to find the ships, follow them, and bring

8
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:21,000
other submarines into the attack.

9
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,160
By the end of May, the balance had

10
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:24,880
changed.

11
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:27,640
Aircraft could reach farther into the

12
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:30,340
ocean, and small carriers brought their

13
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:31,800
own airfields with them.

14
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:34,920
German submarines were suffering losses

15
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:37,280
their commander could not accept.

16
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:40,000
The Allies had made the hunting ground

17
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:42,000
dangerous for the hunters.

18
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:45,520
But closing the gap took more than a

19
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:48,520
bomber with extra fuel. Britain's war

20
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,920
depended on ships arriving. Food and fuel

21
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:54,260
were part of the cargo, but so were the

22
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:56,760
men and equipment needed to build an army

23
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,100
for the return to Europe.

24
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:01,640
A ship sunk in the Atlantic meant more

25
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:04,660
than a missing hull. What it carried would

26
00:01:04,739 --> 00:01:06,880
not be waiting at the other end.

27
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,880
The German aim was to break that movement

28
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:14,340
of supplies. Admiral Karl Dönitz directed

29
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:16,780
a submarine campaign against merchant

30
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:19,660
shipping, trying to concentrate his boats

31
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,560
where they could do the most damage.

32
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:25,020
The Atlantic offered an immense area to

33
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:28,680
search. Finding a convoy and then keeping

34
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:31,760
contact with it was a problem before the

35
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:33,400
first torpedo was fired.

36
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:36,820
For the Allies, bringing merchant ships

37
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:39,180
together allowed escorts to protect a

38
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:43,800
group. Yet a convoy was no fortress. Its

39
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:45,740
ships still had to make the crossing

40
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:48,600
through darkness and bad weather, with

41
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:50,460
submarines trying to reach firing

42
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:51,720
positions around them.

43
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:55,020
The screen of warships could be pressed

44
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:57,760
from more than one direction at once.

45
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:03,640
In March, the convoys known as HX 229 and

46
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:07,680
SC 122 became the center of a running

47
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:08,259
battle.

48
00:02:09,479 --> 00:02:11,420
German boats gathered around them as they

49
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:13,820
crossed the Atlantic. Ships were

50
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:16,560
torpedoed, and the escorts fought to keep

51
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:17,640
the attacks away.

52
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:21,000
The fighting exposed the cost of being

53
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:22,980
beyond reliable air support.

54
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:25,920
The official Royal Air Force history

55
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:29,780
describes one Liberator trying to find SC

56
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:33,620
122 and returning after more than 20 hours

57
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:36,500
in the air. It had failed to locate the

58
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:37,140
convoy.

59
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,400
Reaching far across the sea was an

60
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:43,620
achievement. Finding the right ships in

61
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:45,740
the weather of the North Atlantic was

62
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:47,400
another task altogether.

63
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:50,500
Another account in the same history

64
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:53,280
follows a Liberator flying from Iceland

65
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:55,820
towards HX 229.

66
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:58,880
A strong wind across the runways had

67
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:01,680
delayed its arrival. When it reached the

68
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:04,600
ships, it could cover them for four hours.

69
00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:07,920
The air crew attacked submarines, but the

70
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:10,500
convoy's protection still depended on

71
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:13,420
weather, distance, and the time left

72
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:15,460
before the aircraft had to leave.

73
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:18,860
That is the problem hidden inside the

74
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:22,300
words air gap. It was not simply a shape

75
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:25,620
on a chart waiting to be colored in. It

76
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:27,660
was the distance between an aircraft's

77
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:31,580
base and a moving convoy, the fuel needed

78
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:34,000
to return, and the chance of finding

79
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:36,500
anything when the sea disappeared under

80
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:37,160
cloud.

81
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:41,000
How could the Allies turn occasional help

82
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:43,480
into a more dependable defense?

83
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:46,420
The conventional U-boats fighting this

84
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:48,920
campaign were not designed to spend their

85
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:52,060
whole patrol deep underwater. They used

86
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:54,460
diesel engines on the surface and

87
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:56,200
batteries when submerged.

88
00:03:57,360 --> 00:03:59,940
Going below offered concealment, but it

89
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:03,020
also imposed limits. The batteries had to

90
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:05,160
be charged, and the boats could make

91
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:06,880
better speed on the surface.

92
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:10,340
This made air cover valuable, even when an

93
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:13,100
aircraft did not sink anything. A

94
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:16,380
submarine forced to dive lost the freedom

95
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,040
it had enjoyed above water.

96
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:21,019
It could become harder to keep up with the

97
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:24,060
convoy or move ahead for another attack.

98
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:27,240
An air patrol could disrupt the hunt

99
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:29,240
without leaving a wreck behind.

100
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:32,080
The March fighting gives a specific

101
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:35,740
example. A Sunderland found a U-boat near

102
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:39,520
a tanker that had fallen behind SC 122.

103
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:42,760
The aircraft attacked, and the submarine

104
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:43,640
submerged.

105
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:46,860
In that encounter, protection meant

106
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:48,840
preventing an attack on the tanker.

107
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:52,460
Counting only submarines sunk would miss

108
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:54,120
what the aircraft had done for the

109
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:56,880
merchant ship. The U-boat's method

110
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:00,620
depended on contact spreading. A boat that

111
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:03,380
found a convoy could call others towards

112
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:06,740
it. Those messages were useful to German

113
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:09,620
commanders, but transmitting carried a

114
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:10,200
risk.

115
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:13,480
Radio signals could also tell Allied

116
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:16,420
listeners that a submarine was there and

117
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:18,640
help them work out the direction from

118
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:21,480
which the signal had come. That did not

119
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:23,560
give the Allies a perfect view of the

120
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:26,980
ocean. Information could arrive too late

121
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:28,500
or be incomplete.

122
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:32,040
A convoy could move into danger while

123
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:34,420
headquarters tried to work out where the

124
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:36,940
boats were gathering. Intelligence

125
00:05:37,060 --> 00:05:39,660
mattered, but it still had to become a

126
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:42,740
useful order for a ship or an aircraft at

127
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:43,140
sea.

128
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:47,560
The geography made the task harder. Bases

129
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,340
in Newfoundland, Iceland, and Northern

130
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:53,220
Ireland helped aircraft reach the North

131
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:55,580
Atlantic routes from different sides.

132
00:05:56,220 --> 00:05:59,040
Between their useful patrol areas lay

133
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:02,140
water where convoys could not count on the

134
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:05,568
same protection. The boundaries changed as

135
00:06:05,668 --> 00:06:08,748
aircraft, bases, and operating conditions

136
00:06:08,868 --> 00:06:11,928
changed. There was no single permanent

137
00:06:12,088 --> 00:06:13,448
fence across the sea.

138
00:06:14,308 --> 00:06:16,948
Draw a circle around an airfield, and it

139
00:06:17,008 --> 00:06:19,408
is tempting to call everything inside it

140
00:06:19,528 --> 00:06:19,988
covered.

141
00:06:20,888 --> 00:06:23,168
But a maximum distance is not the same

142
00:06:23,228 --> 00:06:26,368
thing as a working patrol. The aircraft

143
00:06:26,468 --> 00:06:29,268
must reach its search area, spend time

144
00:06:29,328 --> 00:06:32,948
there, and return. A convoy near the edge

145
00:06:33,008 --> 00:06:35,808
of that reach might see an aircraft only

146
00:06:35,828 --> 00:06:39,068
briefly, nor did the absence of aircraft

147
00:06:39,188 --> 00:06:40,908
mean there were no defenders.

148
00:06:41,768 --> 00:06:44,268
Surface escorts traveled with the merchant

149
00:06:44,368 --> 00:06:48,088
ships. They could search and attack, but

150
00:06:48,128 --> 00:06:50,528
their own tasks competed for time,

151
00:06:51,468 --> 00:06:54,008
staying with the convoy, investigating

152
00:06:54,108 --> 00:06:56,828
contacts, and dealing with attacks.

153
00:06:57,608 --> 00:07:00,048
Aircraft added another way of seeing

154
00:07:00,068 --> 00:07:03,388
beyond that immediate screen. The answer,

155
00:07:03,468 --> 00:07:07,208
therefore, had two parts: extend the reach

156
00:07:07,268 --> 00:07:10,188
of aircraft based on land, and bring

157
00:07:10,268 --> 00:07:12,748
aircraft to sea aboard carriers.

158
00:07:13,648 --> 00:07:15,968
Neither would make the Atlantic safe on

159
00:07:16,008 --> 00:07:16,608
its own.

160
00:07:17,468 --> 00:07:19,148
Together, they could deprive the

161
00:07:19,208 --> 00:07:22,488
submarines of some of the room and time on

162
00:07:22,548 --> 00:07:25,848
which their operations depended. The next

163
00:07:25,868 --> 00:07:28,048
difficulty was making those aircraft

164
00:07:28,108 --> 00:07:31,148
available where they were needed. Consider

165
00:07:31,188 --> 00:07:34,088
the difference on the map. A land-based

166
00:07:34,168 --> 00:07:37,068
patrol starts at a fixed point while the

167
00:07:37,148 --> 00:07:39,188
convoy moves across its reach.

168
00:07:40,148 --> 00:07:42,828
A carrier starts much closer to the ships

169
00:07:42,888 --> 00:07:46,308
it supports and moves too. These are

170
00:07:46,348 --> 00:07:49,348
different answers to distance. Drawing

171
00:07:49,448 --> 00:07:51,868
both helps explain why the arrival of

172
00:07:51,928 --> 00:07:55,008
carriers did not make long-range aircraft

173
00:07:55,108 --> 00:07:57,988
redundant, and why greater bomber range

174
00:07:58,328 --> 00:08:00,708
did not remove the value of a carrier.

175
00:08:01,608 --> 00:08:03,908
The Liberator was central to that effort.

176
00:08:04,528 --> 00:08:06,848
Built in America, it was a heavy bomber

177
00:08:07,148 --> 00:08:08,888
that could also serve over the sea.

178
00:08:09,788 --> 00:08:11,827
Very long-range versions helped carry

179
00:08:11,908 --> 00:08:14,168
patrols into the Central Atlantic.

180
00:08:15,168 --> 00:08:17,808
In this role, the important question was

181
00:08:17,908 --> 00:08:20,408
how far a crew could go, search

182
00:08:20,468 --> 00:08:22,788
effectively, and still get home.

183
00:08:23,728 --> 00:08:26,548
Extra fuel was part of the answer. The

184
00:08:26,568 --> 00:08:29,668
official RAF history records long-range

185
00:08:29,788 --> 00:08:33,048
tanks being fitted to Liberators and also

186
00:08:33,088 --> 00:08:35,248
records that the work was slow.

187
00:08:36,168 --> 00:08:38,288
An aircraft did not become a useful

188
00:08:38,388 --> 00:08:40,988
maritime patrol machine simply because

189
00:08:41,048 --> 00:08:43,448
someone assigned it to a new command.

190
00:08:44,308 --> 00:08:46,868
It needed the equipment and preparation

191
00:08:46,928 --> 00:08:47,708
for that work.

192
00:08:49,148 --> 00:08:51,428
There was a second conversion taking place

193
00:08:51,568 --> 00:08:55,248
inside the aircraft. Radar had to help its

194
00:08:55,308 --> 00:08:57,008
crew find submarines.

195
00:08:57,908 --> 00:09:00,508
Installing the right sets took time too.

196
00:09:01,328 --> 00:09:03,428
Range, without a means of finding the

197
00:09:03,488 --> 00:09:06,188
target, would leave an air crew searching

198
00:09:06,228 --> 00:09:09,168
a vast area with limited information.

199
00:09:10,148 --> 00:09:12,568
Better detection, without enough range,

200
00:09:12,948 --> 00:09:14,728
would leave the central problem out of

201
00:09:14,788 --> 00:09:15,208
reach.

202
00:09:16,248 --> 00:09:18,728
The photographs from Aldergrove make that

203
00:09:18,768 --> 00:09:21,628
transition visible. In one official

204
00:09:21,748 --> 00:09:24,468
picture, Liberators of number one hundred

205
00:09:24,488 --> 00:09:27,548
and twenty Squadron stand beside another

206
00:09:27,588 --> 00:09:29,168
from number eighty-six Squadron.

207
00:09:30,048 --> 00:09:32,168
Their radar installations are different.

208
00:09:32,928 --> 00:09:34,988
The machines belong to the same broad

209
00:09:35,128 --> 00:09:37,528
aircraft family, but they did not all

210
00:09:37,608 --> 00:09:39,168
carry the same equipment.

211
00:09:40,248 --> 00:09:42,928
On the ground, the work continued between

212
00:09:42,948 --> 00:09:46,088
patrols. Another photograph from April

213
00:09:46,448 --> 00:09:48,968
shows an armorer cleaning a Liberator's

214
00:09:49,028 --> 00:09:52,488
rear turret during daily inspections. It

215
00:09:52,548 --> 00:09:55,108
is an ordinary maintenance scene, and that

216
00:09:55,148 --> 00:09:56,268
is why it matters here.

217
00:09:57,188 --> 00:10:00,128
A patrol was made possible by work before

218
00:10:00,208 --> 00:10:03,148
takeoff as well as by the crew that flew

219
00:10:03,208 --> 00:10:03,448
it.

220
00:10:04,668 --> 00:10:07,368
Air Marshal John Slessor took charge of

221
00:10:07,408 --> 00:10:10,128
Coastal Command early in 1943.

222
00:10:10,988 --> 00:10:13,488
Admiral Max Horton commanded Western

223
00:10:13,528 --> 00:10:14,248
Approaches.

224
00:10:15,108 --> 00:10:17,028
Their responsibilities met over the

225
00:10:17,088 --> 00:10:20,888
convoys. Naval and air forces had to help

226
00:10:20,928 --> 00:10:23,888
the same ships while working with limited

227
00:10:23,928 --> 00:10:24,648
resources.

228
00:10:25,588 --> 00:10:27,368
The problem was deciding where the

229
00:10:27,408 --> 00:10:29,768
available protection could do the most

230
00:10:29,808 --> 00:10:30,128
good.

231
00:10:31,068 --> 00:10:33,208
The official history describes a daily

232
00:10:33,268 --> 00:10:35,568
telephone conference linking Coastal

233
00:10:35,648 --> 00:10:38,128
Command, Western Approaches, and the

234
00:10:38,188 --> 00:10:40,448
Admiralty's Submarine Intelligence staff.

235
00:10:41,388 --> 00:10:44,048
They used the latest information to choose

236
00:10:44,088 --> 00:10:46,268
which convoys should receive air

237
00:10:46,288 --> 00:10:46,968
protection.

238
00:10:48,028 --> 00:10:50,108
Slessor could not provide it for every

239
00:10:50,228 --> 00:10:53,608
convoy. Concentrating cover on those

240
00:10:53,628 --> 00:10:56,168
thought threatened was a way of using a

241
00:10:56,208 --> 00:10:57,268
scarce resource.

242
00:10:58,388 --> 00:11:00,228
The same problem crossed national

243
00:11:00,328 --> 00:11:03,508
boundaries. British, Canadian, and

244
00:11:03,568 --> 00:11:06,088
American forces shared the Atlantic

245
00:11:06,168 --> 00:11:09,568
battle, but shared purpose did not remove

246
00:11:09,608 --> 00:11:12,128
differences in command and procedure.

247
00:11:13,228 --> 00:11:15,148
A conference in Washington in March

248
00:11:15,588 --> 00:11:18,108
reorganized convoy arrangements and

249
00:11:18,148 --> 00:11:20,468
included commitments to strengthen very

250
00:11:20,568 --> 00:11:22,868
long-range air forces based in

251
00:11:22,908 --> 00:11:23,708
Newfoundland.

252
00:11:24,668 --> 00:11:27,008
Cooperation itself was part of the

253
00:11:27,048 --> 00:11:27,748
defense.

254
00:11:28,728 --> 00:11:31,468
This is why a story about a few remarkable

255
00:11:31,648 --> 00:11:34,748
aircraft must also be a story about

256
00:11:34,828 --> 00:11:35,848
organization.

257
00:11:36,708 --> 00:11:39,388
Fuel tanks and radar sets could make a

258
00:11:39,408 --> 00:11:42,828
patrol possible. Orders, information,

259
00:11:43,268 --> 00:11:46,328
maintenance, and trains crews helped put

260
00:11:46,408 --> 00:11:47,908
it in the right place.

261
00:11:48,868 --> 00:11:51,048
The Liberator mattered because of what

262
00:11:51,108 --> 00:11:54,508
that whole system allowed it to do, and

263
00:11:54,548 --> 00:11:58,088
the system was acquiring a new way to see.

264
00:11:59,088 --> 00:12:01,388
There was no simple dividing line between

265
00:12:01,448 --> 00:12:04,388
invention and operations. The new

266
00:12:04,428 --> 00:12:06,828
equipment had to reach a squadron, be

267
00:12:06,888 --> 00:12:09,048
fitted to an aircraft, and work on a

268
00:12:09,088 --> 00:12:12,352
patrol. The official history records those

269
00:12:12,372 --> 00:12:15,892
delays alongside the successes. They are

270
00:12:15,952 --> 00:12:18,712
part of the explanation for the timing of

271
00:12:18,772 --> 00:12:22,052
the change. A promising machine on paper

272
00:12:22,412 --> 00:12:25,372
was not yet an aircraft above a threatened

273
00:12:25,432 --> 00:12:25,972
convoy.

274
00:12:26,852 --> 00:12:28,732
A U-boat on the surface could be hard to

275
00:12:28,772 --> 00:12:29,592
find at night.

276
00:12:30,492 --> 00:12:33,452
Radar changed that by using radio waves to

277
00:12:33,492 --> 00:12:35,492
detect a target instead of relying

278
00:12:35,532 --> 00:12:38,432
entirely on eyesight. But the Germans

279
00:12:38,472 --> 00:12:40,912
developed a warning receiver called Metox.

280
00:12:41,652 --> 00:12:43,672
It could detect the transmissions from the

281
00:12:43,772 --> 00:12:46,392
earlier Allied radar and give a submarine

282
00:12:46,452 --> 00:12:49,332
time to dive before the aircraft arrived.

283
00:12:50,612 --> 00:12:53,292
That set up a contest between finding and

284
00:12:53,412 --> 00:12:53,872
warning.

285
00:12:55,032 --> 00:12:57,092
A patrol crew wanted to get close enough

286
00:12:57,132 --> 00:12:59,472
for an effective attack. The submarine

287
00:12:59,572 --> 00:13:01,832
crew wanted notice soon enough to

288
00:13:01,872 --> 00:13:02,512
disappear.

289
00:13:03,952 --> 00:13:06,552
The usefulness of radar depended partly on

290
00:13:06,592 --> 00:13:09,412
whether the enemy could hear it coming. In

291
00:13:09,472 --> 00:13:11,712
this struggle, changing the equipment

292
00:13:11,752 --> 00:13:13,892
could change the time available to both

293
00:13:13,992 --> 00:13:14,532
crews.

294
00:13:15,932 --> 00:13:18,612
During nineteen forty-three, centimetric

295
00:13:18,732 --> 00:13:21,692
radar altered that contest. The new

296
00:13:21,772 --> 00:13:24,332
airborne sets operated at much shorter

297
00:13:24,412 --> 00:13:26,392
wavelengths than the earlier equipment.

298
00:13:27,172 --> 00:13:29,372
Metox could not provide the same warning

299
00:13:29,432 --> 00:13:30,052
against them.

300
00:13:30,972 --> 00:13:33,312
A surface submarine could now be detected

301
00:13:33,352 --> 00:13:35,532
without receiving the warning its crew had

302
00:13:35,572 --> 00:13:37,532
come to expect from the old system.

303
00:13:38,812 --> 00:13:40,972
The change did not mean all aircraft were

304
00:13:41,012 --> 00:13:42,352
transformed overnight.

305
00:13:43,212 --> 00:13:45,312
Sets reached different units at different

306
00:13:45,432 --> 00:13:47,752
times, and fitting them was work in

307
00:13:47,812 --> 00:13:48,432
itself.

308
00:13:49,512 --> 00:13:51,892
The RAF history describes the spread

309
00:13:51,912 --> 00:13:53,732
through squadrons during the spring.

310
00:13:54,612 --> 00:13:57,172
This was a developing capability, not a

311
00:13:57,232 --> 00:13:59,152
single switch thrown across the whole

312
00:13:59,192 --> 00:14:00,792
Atlantic on one morning.

313
00:14:02,052 --> 00:14:04,112
Radar could bring an aircraft towards a

314
00:14:04,152 --> 00:14:07,112
contact. The final attack still posed its

315
00:14:07,172 --> 00:14:08,032
own problems.

316
00:14:09,012 --> 00:14:11,432
The Leigh Light was a powerful searchlight

317
00:14:11,832 --> 00:14:13,992
used to illuminate a submarine at night.

318
00:14:14,812 --> 00:14:16,872
Combined with radar that did not give the

319
00:14:16,932 --> 00:14:19,732
old warning, it offered a way to find and

320
00:14:19,771 --> 00:14:21,832
attack a boat during the hours when

321
00:14:21,872 --> 00:14:23,692
darkness had helped conceal it.

322
00:14:24,832 --> 00:14:26,692
Radio direction finding worked

323
00:14:26,732 --> 00:14:27,312
differently.

324
00:14:28,212 --> 00:14:30,052
Rather than sending out a pulse and

325
00:14:30,112 --> 00:14:32,372
looking for an echo, it listened for an

326
00:14:32,432 --> 00:14:33,572
enemy transmission.

327
00:14:34,712 --> 00:14:37,312
High-frequency direction finding was often

328
00:14:37,412 --> 00:14:40,612
called Huff Duff. A bearing could direct

329
00:14:40,652 --> 00:14:42,312
attention towards a transmitting

330
00:14:42,432 --> 00:14:43,132
submarine.

331
00:14:44,132 --> 00:14:46,212
It was useful even without reading the

332
00:14:46,252 --> 00:14:47,412
words of the message.

333
00:14:48,312 --> 00:14:50,932
That distinction matters. Breaking an

334
00:14:50,972 --> 00:14:53,512
encrypted message, finding the direction

335
00:14:53,552 --> 00:14:56,172
of a radio signal, and detecting a target

336
00:14:56,212 --> 00:14:58,192
with radar were different methods.

337
00:14:59,052 --> 00:15:01,172
They could contribute to the same campaign

338
00:15:01,232 --> 00:15:04,032
without being interchangeable. None should

339
00:15:04,072 --> 00:15:06,292
be drawn as an all-seeing instrument that

340
00:15:06,312 --> 00:15:08,872
placed every submarine precisely on an

341
00:15:08,932 --> 00:15:09,852
Allied chart.

342
00:15:11,112 --> 00:15:14,052
On a diagram, one radio bearing is a

343
00:15:14,092 --> 00:15:16,392
direction, not an exact point.

344
00:15:17,312 --> 00:15:19,392
Bearings from different positions can help

345
00:15:19,512 --> 00:15:21,832
narrow an area, but that is still not the

346
00:15:21,892 --> 00:15:24,352
same thing as an aircraft identifying a

347
00:15:24,392 --> 00:15:25,652
boat on the surface.

348
00:15:26,632 --> 00:15:28,652
Each piece of information has its own

349
00:15:28,712 --> 00:15:31,692
limits. The practical value comes from

350
00:15:31,792 --> 00:15:34,392
using it to decide where to look and what

351
00:15:34,432 --> 00:15:35,392
to do next.

352
00:15:36,712 --> 00:15:39,612
At sea, improved detection gave escorts

353
00:15:39,772 --> 00:15:41,212
more chances to act.

354
00:15:42,132 --> 00:15:44,332
Surface radar could help find a submarine

355
00:15:44,372 --> 00:15:47,452
above water. Sonar was used to search

356
00:15:47,472 --> 00:15:50,712
below it. Weapons and tactics had to turn

357
00:15:50,772 --> 00:15:52,632
that contact into an attack.

358
00:15:53,612 --> 00:15:56,152
The machinery was important, but so was

359
00:15:56,192 --> 00:15:58,232
the work of crews interpreting it and

360
00:15:58,332 --> 00:15:59,512
handling their ships.

361
00:16:00,512 --> 00:16:02,472
By the spring of nineteen forty-three,

362
00:16:02,852 --> 00:16:04,592
these changes were coming together.

363
00:16:05,592 --> 00:16:07,772
Longer reach helped aircraft get there.

364
00:16:08,472 --> 00:16:11,392
Radar helped them search. Direction

365
00:16:11,512 --> 00:16:13,912
finding exploited the transmissions needed

366
00:16:13,952 --> 00:16:15,312
to organize attacks.

367
00:16:16,452 --> 00:16:18,672
Surface escorts remained beside the

368
00:16:18,712 --> 00:16:21,612
merchantmen. Yet the test of this growing

369
00:16:21,752 --> 00:16:23,812
system came in weather that made one of

370
00:16:23,852 --> 00:16:26,372
its strongest elements difficult to use.

371
00:16:27,352 --> 00:16:30,932
The convoy was ONS 5, sailing westwards

372
00:16:30,992 --> 00:16:33,892
across the North Atlantic. Its battle ran

373
00:16:33,912 --> 00:16:36,592
from late April into early May. German

374
00:16:36,692 --> 00:16:38,592
submarines converged on the merchant

375
00:16:38,652 --> 00:16:41,112
ships, and the escorts faced repeated

376
00:16:41,152 --> 00:16:43,952
attacks. This was not a demonstration in

377
00:16:43,992 --> 00:16:46,272
controlled conditions. It was another

378
00:16:46,312 --> 00:16:48,032
convoy trying to get through difficult

379
00:16:48,172 --> 00:16:50,072
seas while being hunted.

380
00:16:51,032 --> 00:16:53,652
Weather shaped the fighting. Storms and

381
00:16:53,772 --> 00:16:56,092
poor visibility complicated the movement

382
00:16:56,152 --> 00:16:59,192
of ships and aircraft alike. A patrol

383
00:16:59,232 --> 00:17:01,272
assigned to help the convoy was not

384
00:17:01,312 --> 00:17:04,212
guaranteed to find it, nor could air crews

385
00:17:04,272 --> 00:17:06,912
simply ignore conditions at their bases or

386
00:17:06,992 --> 00:17:09,712
over the search area. The gap between an

387
00:17:09,812 --> 00:17:12,372
order and useful support could be

388
00:17:12,452 --> 00:17:13,632
painfully large.

389
00:17:14,592 --> 00:17:16,472
The Canadian official air history

390
00:17:16,832 --> 00:17:18,892
describes a Liberator from Iceland

391
00:17:19,232 --> 00:17:22,672
reaching ONS 5 more than a thousand miles

392
00:17:22,712 --> 00:17:23,512
from its base.

393
00:17:24,372 --> 00:17:26,332
The crew saw nothing through the patches

394
00:17:26,372 --> 00:17:29,512
of fog during its short time there. That

395
00:17:29,552 --> 00:17:32,112
is a useful measure of both achievement

396
00:17:32,512 --> 00:17:35,232
and limitation. The aircraft could make

397
00:17:35,252 --> 00:17:38,172
the journey, but distance alone could not

398
00:17:38,192 --> 00:17:39,572
make the patrol effective.

399
00:17:40,492 --> 00:17:42,672
When thick fog settled over the battle,

400
00:17:43,172 --> 00:17:45,292
surface escorts with radar gained an

401
00:17:45,332 --> 00:17:47,472
advantage over submarines trying to

402
00:17:47,512 --> 00:17:50,932
attack. The Canadian history identifies

403
00:17:51,052 --> 00:17:53,752
weather and modern shipborne radar as

404
00:17:53,792 --> 00:17:55,972
decisive factors in this encounter.

405
00:17:57,012 --> 00:17:58,892
Aircraft had contributed to the wider

406
00:17:59,012 --> 00:18:01,232
struggle, but they should not be credited

407
00:18:01,272 --> 00:18:03,972
with fighting a surface action through fog

408
00:18:04,292 --> 00:18:06,372
that restricted their own operations.

409
00:18:07,492 --> 00:18:10,172
Merchant ships were lost. The convoy's

410
00:18:10,232 --> 00:18:13,572
passage was not a bloodless success, and a

411
00:18:13,612 --> 00:18:16,012
change in the campaign did not lessen the

412
00:18:16,072 --> 00:18:18,372
cost to those aboard the ships that were

413
00:18:18,432 --> 00:18:18,772
hit.

414
00:18:19,716 --> 00:18:21,856
But the U-boats also suffered serious

415
00:18:21,956 --> 00:18:25,236
losses. Attacking a convoy was becoming an

416
00:18:25,276 --> 00:18:28,096
increasingly expensive way to try to cut

417
00:18:28,156 --> 00:18:29,196
the Atlantic route.

418
00:18:30,316 --> 00:18:33,376
ONS 5 matters because it complicates an

419
00:18:33,436 --> 00:18:36,516
easy answer. If the story were only that

420
00:18:36,596 --> 00:18:38,936
longer range bombers arrived and defeated

421
00:18:38,976 --> 00:18:41,176
the submarines, this battle would be

422
00:18:41,216 --> 00:18:44,616
difficult to explain. It instead shows why

423
00:18:44,656 --> 00:18:46,736
the Allies needed several forms of

424
00:18:46,776 --> 00:18:49,656
protection. When aircraft were limited,

425
00:18:50,056 --> 00:18:52,516
the ships and their equipment still had to

426
00:18:52,556 --> 00:18:52,956
fight.

427
00:18:53,996 --> 00:18:58,336
Later in May, SC 130 provided another kind

428
00:18:58,376 --> 00:19:01,076
of evidence. The convoy passed through a

429
00:19:01,136 --> 00:19:03,936
large concentration of submarines without

430
00:19:03,976 --> 00:19:07,016
losing a merchant ship. Coastal command

431
00:19:07,096 --> 00:19:09,356
aircraft made repeated sightings.

432
00:19:10,236 --> 00:19:13,096
In the RAF history, a Liberator of number

433
00:19:13,376 --> 00:19:16,756
120 Squadron recorded eight sightings

434
00:19:16,836 --> 00:19:19,976
during a single sortie. A sighting was not

435
00:19:20,036 --> 00:19:22,676
a sinking, and eight sightings did not

436
00:19:22,736 --> 00:19:25,636
mean eight boats destroyed. But it showed

437
00:19:25,676 --> 00:19:27,916
the attention aircraft could bring to the

438
00:19:27,976 --> 00:19:30,296
waters around a threatened convoy.

439
00:19:31,136 --> 00:19:33,416
Submarines faced pressure before reaching

440
00:19:33,456 --> 00:19:35,956
the ships, while escorts guarded the

441
00:19:35,996 --> 00:19:38,776
closer approaches. The defenders were

442
00:19:38,796 --> 00:19:41,636
becoming harder to separate into isolated

443
00:19:41,676 --> 00:19:42,276
problems.

444
00:19:43,216 --> 00:19:45,176
This was also a change in what a

445
00:19:45,216 --> 00:19:47,096
successful defense looked like.

446
00:19:48,036 --> 00:19:50,576
A convoy that arrived without loss could

447
00:19:50,616 --> 00:19:52,376
be the most important result of the

448
00:19:52,416 --> 00:19:54,856
battle, even when the story lacked a

449
00:19:54,896 --> 00:19:57,416
dramatic sinking to put at its center.

450
00:19:58,536 --> 00:20:02,736
SC 130 makes that point plainly. Its

451
00:20:02,796 --> 00:20:05,696
merchant ships got through. The cargos,

452
00:20:06,116 --> 00:20:08,336
rather than a tally of attacks, were the

453
00:20:08,396 --> 00:20:09,756
purpose of the protection.

454
00:20:10,876 --> 00:20:13,556
Small escort carriers added another layer.

455
00:20:14,316 --> 00:20:16,876
Ships such as the American Bogue carried

456
00:20:16,936 --> 00:20:19,756
aircraft into the ocean rather than making

457
00:20:19,836 --> 00:20:23,416
every patrol begin at a distant shore. The

458
00:20:23,476 --> 00:20:25,376
carrier moved the starting point of the

459
00:20:25,416 --> 00:20:27,956
search. Its aircraft did not need the

460
00:20:27,996 --> 00:20:30,756
reach of a land-based Liberator to operate

461
00:20:30,816 --> 00:20:32,676
around ships already nearby.

462
00:20:33,556 --> 00:20:35,776
The two approaches reinforced each other.

463
00:20:36,536 --> 00:20:39,316
Land-based patrol aircraft extended their

464
00:20:39,356 --> 00:20:42,356
reach from the edges. Carrier aircraft

465
00:20:42,716 --> 00:20:44,716
worked from a moving base at sea.

466
00:20:45,676 --> 00:20:47,316
Surface escorts stayed with the

467
00:20:47,376 --> 00:20:50,436
merchantmen. The Atlantic was still vast,

468
00:20:50,836 --> 00:20:53,196
but the submarines had fewer opportunities

469
00:20:53,556 --> 00:20:55,956
to rely on a convoy passing beyond the

470
00:20:55,996 --> 00:20:58,516
reach of aircraft for a long stretch.

471
00:20:59,476 --> 00:21:01,536
On the twenty-fourth of May, Donitz

472
00:21:01,596 --> 00:21:03,136
ordered his boats away from the North

473
00:21:03,176 --> 00:21:06,016
Atlantic convoy battle. The losses have

474
00:21:06,056 --> 00:21:07,776
made continuing the offensive on those

475
00:21:07,836 --> 00:21:09,136
terms unacceptable.

476
00:21:10,036 --> 00:21:12,456
It was the decisive reversal remembered as

477
00:21:12,496 --> 00:21:14,816
Black May. The commander who had

478
00:21:14,856 --> 00:21:16,476
concentrated submarines against the

479
00:21:16,516 --> 00:21:18,896
convoys now had to seek less dangerous

480
00:21:18,996 --> 00:21:20,376
areas for operations.

481
00:21:21,576 --> 00:21:23,476
It was not the end of submarine warfare.

482
00:21:24,196 --> 00:21:26,676
U-boats continued to operate and ships and

483
00:21:26,756 --> 00:21:29,596
crews continued to be lost. Nor had the

484
00:21:29,656 --> 00:21:31,576
Allies created an unbroken roof of

485
00:21:31,656 --> 00:21:34,496
aircraft above every convoy. Weather,

486
00:21:34,956 --> 00:21:36,956
serviceability, and distance still

487
00:21:37,036 --> 00:21:37,516
mattered.

488
00:21:38,396 --> 00:21:40,216
What had changed was the balance of

489
00:21:40,256 --> 00:21:42,596
advantage on the principal North Atlantic

490
00:21:42,696 --> 00:21:43,576
convoy routes.

491
00:21:44,556 --> 00:21:47,516
The phrase "closing the gap" is useful if

492
00:21:47,556 --> 00:21:50,076
it describes that change. It becomes

493
00:21:50,116 --> 00:21:52,136
misleading if it suggests that a fixed

494
00:21:52,216 --> 00:21:54,916
patch of ocean became perfectly watched at

495
00:21:54,956 --> 00:21:58,156
a precise instant. Air coverage expanded

496
00:21:58,176 --> 00:22:01,036
through aircraft, bases, and carriers. The

497
00:22:01,076 --> 00:22:03,256
operational effect grew from their use

498
00:22:03,276 --> 00:22:05,776
together, alongside the escorts and the

499
00:22:05,796 --> 00:22:07,156
information directing them.

500
00:22:08,096 --> 00:22:10,136
The same care is needed with the familiar

501
00:22:10,236 --> 00:22:13,096
loss totals. Accounts do not always count

502
00:22:13,136 --> 00:22:15,636
the same waters, the same causes, or the

503
00:22:15,696 --> 00:22:19,016
same period. For this story, the essential

504
00:22:19,156 --> 00:22:21,676
point is the decision those losses forced.

505
00:22:22,336 --> 00:22:24,096
The submarine command could no longer

506
00:22:24,156 --> 00:22:26,536
sustain its spring offensive against the

507
00:22:26,576 --> 00:22:28,676
North Atlantic convoys on the existing

508
00:22:28,776 --> 00:22:29,356
terms.

509
00:22:30,336 --> 00:22:32,136
It would also be misleading to give all

510
00:22:32,176 --> 00:22:35,196
the credit to a single invention. A radar

511
00:22:35,316 --> 00:22:38,056
set could not deliver a cargo. An escort

512
00:22:38,096 --> 00:22:40,835
could not be everywhere around a convoy. A

513
00:22:40,895 --> 00:22:42,996
long-range aircraft might arrive and see

514
00:22:43,056 --> 00:22:43,556
nothing.

515
00:22:44,436 --> 00:22:46,116
Each answered a different part of the

516
00:22:46,156 --> 00:22:48,656
problem, and each depended on people

517
00:22:48,696 --> 00:22:51,036
making it work in difficult conditions.

518
00:22:52,256 --> 00:22:53,796
The contribution of air power was

519
00:22:53,856 --> 00:22:56,856
nevertheless fundamental. An aircraft

520
00:22:56,896 --> 00:22:59,356
could make a submarine dive, interrupt its

521
00:22:59,396 --> 00:23:01,856
contact with a convoy, or attack it before

522
00:23:01,896 --> 00:23:03,376
it reached the merchant ships.

523
00:23:04,416 --> 00:23:06,656
Very long-range Liberators carried that

524
00:23:06,676 --> 00:23:08,216
pressure farther from shore.

525
00:23:09,116 --> 00:23:10,876
Escort carriers brought it from another

526
00:23:10,896 --> 00:23:13,336
direction with an airfield that traveled

527
00:23:13,376 --> 00:23:14,196
across the sea.

528
00:23:15,256 --> 00:23:16,876
For the people in the merchant ships,

529
00:23:17,236 --> 00:23:20,216
success was an arrival. The cargo reached

530
00:23:20,256 --> 00:23:22,796
its destination. Another crossing could

531
00:23:22,836 --> 00:23:23,276
begin.

532
00:23:24,276 --> 00:23:26,176
The strategic result was the continued

533
00:23:26,296 --> 00:23:28,436
movement on which Britain and the Allied

534
00:23:28,536 --> 00:23:31,556
buildup depended. The convoy system did

535
00:23:31,596 --> 00:23:33,796
not have to destroy every submarine to

536
00:23:33,856 --> 00:23:35,276
keep that movement alive.

537
00:23:36,496 --> 00:23:38,596
Return to the map of the North Atlantic.

538
00:23:39,296 --> 00:23:41,036
At the beginning of the story, the

539
00:23:41,116 --> 00:23:43,096
aircraft turn back while the ships

540
00:23:43,116 --> 00:23:46,596
continue into the gap. By the end, the map

541
00:23:46,656 --> 00:23:49,376
contains more ways to reach them, to find

542
00:23:49,416 --> 00:23:51,456
the attackers, and to bring help.

543
00:23:52,396 --> 00:23:54,556
That is how the Allies closed the U-boat's

544
00:23:54,636 --> 00:23:57,436
safest hunting ground, by changing what

545
00:23:57,456 --> 00:24:00,496
could happen around a convoy far from

546
00:24:00,556 --> 00:24:00,976
land.
